dn't be a lost dinosaur but  rather a dinosaur-like offshoot of a  modern genus taking inspiration from the  Galapagos tortoise they picture the  creature to be a turtle offshoot that  evolved into a giraffe like herbivore in  an isolated island environment while not  quite a dinosaur or a giant super neck  tortoise would be quite the sight to  behold  arguably one of the most fascinating  episodes in cryptozoological history  comes from the Colombian Venezuelan  Border in 1929. here Swiss geologist  Francois delois took a creepy photo of a  deceased primate he claimed was an  Undiscovered species the infamous photo  was treated with immediate skepticism  and has been largely proven to be a  white-faced spider monkey that delois  propped up to make it appear larger than  it actually was but what if the deloy's  ape had been a real animal the team  envisions a primate that split off from  spider monkeys during the pleistocene  becoming large bipedal Ground Dwellers  in our timeline this lineage includes  some of the most intelligent and  adaptable primates so it follows that  the larger delois ape would be  particularly smart perhaps routinely  using Tools in seeming almost human-like  when forging and using weapons  one of my absolute favorite aquatic  Cryptids supposedly dwells in the waters  of the North Atlantic the long-necked  seal most reports come from the 18th  century and describe seal-like creatures  with peculiar elongated necks the scarce  sightings of this creature seem to come  from individuals who were unfamiliar  with the appearance of sea lions the  team envisions long neck seals as a  genus of pinnipeds that fill a unique  ecological niche instead of pursuing  fish in the Open Water these creatures  munch on slow-moving sea cucumbers and  starfish on the abyssal plain their  nostrils have evolved into protruding  breathing snorkels meaning that unlike  common seals long neck seals never have  to come ashore  off the coast of Alaska another aquatic 